#{[17592186562450 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562450 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99476" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562450 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562450 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ 13 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562450 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7eede828-b363-5824-aef0-9e1007418c88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562450 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 13 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=2\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ 13 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5-\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 13 & -5-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(5-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}+(-5-\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(5-\\text{i}){x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(5-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{13}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562465 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562465 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99331" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562465 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562465 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562465 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9b0cd7b7-a43d-5a47-86ed-f4c8db97a17e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562465 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-1$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=3.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i}=-1+3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -1+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -1+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562511 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562511 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140493" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562511 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562511 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562511 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f594f319-f018-5c1b-a5cc-5800c5d009c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562511 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 4\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=4$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =4+(-2)\\text{i}=4-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ -4+(-1)\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ -4-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -4∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562518 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562518 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99343" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562518 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562518 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-4+3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3-2\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562518 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fed17ade-b98f-5b7e-aa56-3747d2eae8ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562518 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}-4+3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
